48	 * limit); a clerk settling one above it makes it PENDING instead. A steward settles any claim, and
49	 * (as may the person's approver) approves a PENDING claim (SETTLED, its due fixed then) or rejects it with a reason (back to OPEN);
50	 * the user who opened a claim cannot approve it. The user who last edited a claim (through its form or
51	 * its row's amount) can neither settle nor approve it, as its opener cannot. A PENDING claim is bound by a statement
52	 * like any other claim that counts: while a statement binds it, it shows the bound figure. An overdue claim's due gains a late charge on its
53	 * figure raised to the floor (see {@link LateCharge}): the floor is applied first, so a claim's live due is its floored
54	 * figure plus the charge on it. A statement binds the floored figure, and settling fixes the charge into the settled due. A claim with
55	 * a negative amount is a correction: it is allowed only when it names, by number, a SETTLED claim
56	 * of the same person that it corrects. Its due has no floor and no late charge. A steward can move
57	 * a DRAFT, OPEN or PENDING claim to another person: it keeps its date, takes the next number (a
58	 * DRAFT, having none, gets none), loses any figure
59	 * a statement bound it to, and is listed as its new person's newest claim. A steward can re-settle
60	 * a SETTLED claim: its due is fixed again at the figure it would have today as an open claim bound
61	 * by no statement (today's rates, grades, floor and late charge). An OPEN claim can be deferred to
62	 * a date after the sheet's today: until the sheet's today reaches it the claim counts in no total,
63	 * is not overdue, cannot be settled and is left unbound by a statement; from then on it is an OPEN
64	 * claim like any other. A claim has a proofs count, raised by one at a time while it is not SETTLED
65	 * or VOID. An OPEN claim can be split: an amount split off it becomes a new OPEN claim (see
66	 * {@link #split}). A claim whose figure is above the proof threshold in force on its date cannot be settled
67	 * or approved while it has no proofs: that is refused before the clerk limit is considered. A claim
68	 * whose due is above the note threshold in force on its date cannot be opened while its note is empty.
69	 * A claim whose due is above the review threshold in force on its date goes to REVIEW when opened,
70	 * instead of OPEN: it records who opened it but takes no number, counts in no total, and cannot be
71	 * edited, settled or split; it can be voided. A steward's review makes it OPEN, numbered then (see
72	 * {@link #review}).
73	 * Who may open, settle, void or move a claim is the sheet's permission matrix (see
74	 * {@link Sheet#permits}): an action the user's role may not take is refused (403). When a claim is
75	 * settled, approved or re-settled, each user watching its person is notified, in the order they
76	 * started watching. A steward may void a SETTLED claim within {@value #VOID_SETTLED_DAYS} days of its
77	 * settlement (the date its status log records it SETTLED) by the sheet's today: it keeps its number and
78	 * its frozen figure, which leaves every total. A DRAFT claim can be deleted (DELETED: it counts nowhere
79	 * and cannot be edited, opened or voided), and restored to DRAFT on the sheet-today it was deleted.
80	 */
81	@Service
82	public class ClaimService {
